/* data/detail-c/mth-12-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/mth-12-08.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-08"] = {"mth-12-08-m01":{"idea":"Chain rule: differentiate the outside, then multiply by the derivative of the inside.","steps":["The outer function is sin u, with derivative cos u.","The inner function is u = x², with derivative 2x.","Multiply them: 2x cos(x²)."],"others":"2x sin(x²) keeps sin instead of changing it to cos. cos(x²) forgets the inner derivative. −2x cos(x²) has a wrong sign, since sin differentiates to +cos.","trap":"Never forget the inner derivative.","answer":"2x cos(x²)"},"mth-12-08-m02":{"idea":"For e raised to a linear power, the exponent's coefficient comes out in front.","steps":["d/dx e^u = e^u × du/dx.","Here u = 3x, so du/dx = 3.","The derivative is 3e^(3x)."],"others":"3xe^(3x − 1) wrongly uses the power rule. e^(3x) alone drops the factor 3. e^(3x)/3 divides instead of multiplying (that is the integral).","trap":"Differentiate gives times 3, integrate gives divide by 3.","answer":"3e^(3x)"},"mth-12-08-m03":{"idea":"This is a basic result to remember.","steps":["The derivative of ln x is 1/x, for x > 0.","By the chain rule, d/dx ln f(x) = f'(x)/f(x).","For f(x) = x, f'(x) = 1, so the result is 1/x."],"others":"1/x² is the derivative of −1/x. ln x/x is not a derivative of ln x. x is the derivative of x²/2.","trap":"Log differentiates to a reciprocal.","answer":"1/x"},"mth-12-08-m04":{"idea":"The derivative of ln f is f' divided by f.","steps":["Here f = sin x, so f' = cos x.","f'/f = cos x/sin x.","That is cot x."],"others":"−cot x has a wrong sign. cos x/ln x puts ln in the denominator. tan x is the reciprocal of the right answer.","trap":"cos over sin is cot, not tan.","answer":"cot x"},"mth-12-08-m05":{"idea":"Use the inverse function rule on y = tan⁻¹x.","steps":["y = tan⁻¹x means x = tan y.","dx/dy = sec²y = 1 + tan²y = 1 + x².","The derivative dy/dx is the reciprocal: 1/(1 + x²)."],"others":"−1/(1 + x²) is the derivative of cot⁻¹x. 1/√(1 − x²) is for sin⁻¹x. 1/(1 − x²) has a minus sign in the wrong place.","trap":"tan⁻¹ is positive, cot⁻¹ is negative.","answer":"1/(1 + x²)"},"mth-12-08-m06":{"idea":"Use the inverse function rule on y = sin⁻¹x.","steps":["y = sin⁻¹x means x = sin y.","dx/dy = cos y = √(1 − sin²y) = √(1 − x²), where cos y is positive on the principal range.","So dy/dx = 1/√(1 − x²)."],"others":"−1/√(1 − x²) is for cos⁻¹x. 1/√(1 + x²) uses the wrong sign under the root. 1/(1 + x²) belongs to tan⁻¹x.","trap":"sin⁻¹ positive, cos⁻¹ negative.","answer":"1/√(1 − x²)"},"mth-12-08-m07":{"idea":"sin⁻¹x and cos⁻¹x add up to a constant, so their derivatives cancel.","steps":["sin⁻¹x + cos⁻¹x = π/2 for all x in [−1, 1].","The derivative of a constant is zero.","So the derivative of cos⁻¹x is the negative of that of sin⁻¹x: −1/√(1 − x²)."],"others":"−1/(1 + x²) is for cot⁻¹x. −1/√(x² − 1) has the wrong expression under the root. 1/√(1 − x²) has lost the minus sign.","trap":"Co-function gets the minus sign.","answer":"−1/√(1 − x²)"},"mth-12-08-m08":{"idea":"Write aˣ with base e, then use the chain rule.","steps":["aˣ = e^(x ln a).","Differentiate: e^(x ln a) × ln a.","That is aˣ ln a. For a = e this gives eˣ because ln e = 1."],"others":"aˣ/ln a divides, which is what happens on integrating. aˣ alone is true only for base e. x aˣ⁻¹ wrongly uses the power rule on a variable exponent.","trap":"Variable exponent: no power rule.","answer":"aˣ ln a"},"mth-12-08-m09":{"idea":"The power rule brings the power down and lowers it by one.","steps":["Multiply by the power n.","Reduce the power by 1.","So the derivative is nxⁿ⁻¹."],"others":"xⁿ⁻¹/n divides by n instead of multiplying. xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) is the integral. nxⁿ keeps the power the same.","trap":"Derivative lowers the power, integral raises it.","answer":"nxⁿ⁻¹"},"mth-12-08-m10":{"idea":"Product rule: each function times the derivative of the other, added.","steps":["Keep u and differentiate v: u dv/dx.","Keep v and differentiate u: v du/dx.","Add them. There is no minus sign."],"others":"The version with a minus sign is part of the quotient rule. The expression over v² is the quotient rule. The product of the two derivatives is a common mistake.","trap":"Product rule uses a plus sign.","answer":"u dv/dx + v du/dx"},"mth-12-08-m11":{"idea":"Quotient rule: bottom times derivative of top, minus top times derivative of bottom, over bottom squared.","steps":["Numerator: v du/dx − u dv/dx.","Denominator: v².","Reversing the numerator changes the sign, so the order matters."],"others":"The ratio of derivatives is not the rule. The reversed numerator has the wrong sign. The version with a plus sign is the product rule on top of v².","trap":"Low d-high minus high d-low.","answer":"(v du/dx − u dv/dx)/v²"},"mth-12-08-m12":{"idea":"The derivative is the limit of chord slopes, which is the tangent slope.","steps":["Take two points on the curve and join them with a chord.","As the second point moves toward the first, the chord becomes the tangent.","So dy/dx at a point is the slope of the tangent there."],"others":"The value of y is just the height. The length of the curve needs an integral. The area under the curve also needs an integral.","trap":"Derivative is slope, integral is area.","answer":"the slope of the tangent to the curve at that point"},"mth-12-08-m13":{"idea":"The normal is perpendicular to the tangent.","steps":["Perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is −1.","If the tangent slope is m, the normal slope is −1/m.","If m = 0 the normal is vertical."],"others":"1/m is only the reciprocal and lacks the minus sign. m is the tangent's own slope. −m only flips the sign.","trap":"Reciprocal and change sign.","answer":"−1/m"},"mth-12-08-m14":{"idea":"Logarithms turn powers into products, which are easier to differentiate.","steps":["Take ln of both sides of y = f(x)^g(x), so ln y = g(x) ln f(x).","Differentiate both sides, with (1/y) dy/dx on the left.","Multiply by y at the end. This is needed when both the base and the exponent change."],"others":"A polynomial can be differentiated directly with the power rule. A linear function has a constant derivative. A constant function has derivative zero.","trap":"Use it when the exponent has x.","answer":"a function like xˣ or (sin x)^(cos x), in which both the base and the exponent change"},"mth-12-08-m15":{"idea":"Use logarithmic differentiation.","steps":["ln y = x ln x.","Differentiate: (1/y) dy/dx = ln x + 1.","So dy/dx = xˣ(1 + ln x)."],"others":"xˣ(1 − ln x) has a wrong sign. x · xˣ⁻¹ uses the power rule, but the exponent is not constant. xˣ ln x is missing the second part from the product rule.","trap":"Product rule on x ln x gives ln x + 1.","answer":"xˣ(1 + ln x)"},"mth-12-08-m16":{"idea":"Differentiate both sides with respect to x, treating y as a function of x.","steps":["Differentiate x² + y² = 25 to get 2x + 2y dy/dx = 0.","Solve for dy/dx: dy/dx = −x/y.","At (3, 4) this gives −3/4."],"others":"x/y misses the minus sign. y/x swaps the variables. −y/x also swaps them.","trap":"Slope of a circle is minus x over y.","answer":"−x/y"},"mth-12-08-m17":{"idea":"y depends on x, so the chain rule applies.","steps":["Differentiate y³ as a power: 3y².","Multiply by the derivative of the inside, dy/dx.","The result is 3y² dy/dx."],"others":"3x² treats the variable as x. y³ dy/dx does not lower the power. 3y² forgets the factor dy/dx.","trap":"Every y term gets a dy/dx.","answer":"3y² dy/dx"},"mth-12-08-m18":{"idea":"Use the chain rule with the parameter t.","steps":["dy/dt = (dy/dx)(dx/dt).","Divide both sides by dx/dt.","So dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt), as long as dx/dt is not zero."],"others":"A difference of derivatives has no meaning here. A product would give the wrong units. The inverted ratio gives dx/dy.","trap":"y-rate over x-rate.","answer":"(dy/dt)/(dx/dt), provided dx/dt is not zero"},"mth-12-08-m19":{"idea":"Differentiate each coordinate with respect to θ, then divide.","steps":["dy/dθ = a cosθ and dx/dθ = −a sinθ.","dy/dx = (a cosθ)/(−a sinθ).","That is −cotθ."],"others":"cotθ drops the minus sign from dx/dθ. −tanθ and tanθ swap sine and cosine.","trap":"Watch the sign from dx/dθ.","answer":"−cotθ"},"mth-12-08-m20":{"idea":"A second derivative is a derivative of a derivative.","steps":["First find dy/dx.","Differentiate it again with respect to x.","The result is d²y/dx², also written y'' or f''(x)."],"others":"Multiplying by x is not differentiating. A reciprocal is not a derivative. The square of the first derivative is not the same as the second derivative.","trap":"d² is not a square.","answer":"the derivative of dy/dx with respect to x"},"mth-12-08-m21":{"idea":"Differentiate twice and add.","steps":["y' = cos x.","y'' = −sin x.","y'' + y = −sin x + sin x = 0."],"others":"sin x would come from y'' = 0. 2 sin x needs y'' = sin x. cos x is the first derivative, not the sum.","trap":"Sine and cosine solve y'' + y = 0.","answer":"0"},"mth-12-08-m22":{"idea":"Write sec x as 1/cos x and use the chain rule.","steps":["sec x = (cos x)⁻¹.","Differentiate: −(cos x)⁻² × (−sin x) = sin x/cos²x.","That equals sec x tan x."],"others":"cosec x cot x is for cosec. −sec x tan x has an extra minus sign. sec²x is the derivative of tan x.","trap":"Only co-functions get a minus sign.","answer":"sec x tan x"},"mth-12-08-m23":{"idea":"Differentiable implies continuous, but not the other way round.","steps":["If the derivative exists at a point, the function has no jump there, so it is continuous.","The reverse is false.","Example: |x| is continuous at 0 but has a sharp corner, so no derivative."],"others":"The claim that every continuous function is differentiable is false. Differentiable functions cannot be discontinuous. A tangent needs continuity first.","trap":"Corner means continuous but not differentiable.","answer":"continuous, but a continuous function need not be differentiable"},"mth-12-08-m24":{"idea":"A constant does not change, so its rate of change is zero.","steps":["The graph of f(x) = 7 is a horizontal line.","A horizontal line has slope 0 everywhere.","So f'(x) = 0."],"others":"x and 1 are derivatives of other functions. 7 would be the value of the function, not the slope.","trap":"Constants vanish on differentiating.","answer":"0"},"mth-12-08-m25":{"idea":"Substitute x = sinθ to simplify.","steps":["Put x = sinθ, so √(1 − x²) = cosθ.","Then 2x√(1 − x²) = 2 sinθ cosθ = sin 2θ, and sin⁻¹ of it is 2θ.","So the function is 2 sin⁻¹x, and the derivative is 2/√(1 − x²)."],"others":"1/√(1 − x²) forgets the factor 2. 2/(1 + x²) uses the tan⁻¹ result. −2/√(1 − x²) has a wrong sign.","trap":"Double the angle, double the derivative.","answer":"2/√(1 − x²)"},"mth-12-08-n01":{"idea":"Use the chain rule, then put in x = 1.","steps":["f'(x) = 2x cos(x²).","At x = 1: f'(1) = 2 cos 1.","cos 1 = 0.5403, so f'(1) = 1.081."],"trap":"The angle is in radians.","answer":"1.081"},"mth-12-08-n02":{"idea":"Use the product rule.","steps":["f'(x) = e^x + x e^x = e^x(1 + x).","At x = 0: e⁰ = 1 and (1 + 0) = 1.","So f'(0) = 1."],"trap":"Both terms of the product rule are needed.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-08-n03":{"idea":"The slope of the tangent is the derivative at that point.","steps":["dy/dx = 3x² − 3.","At x = 2: 3(4) − 3 = 12 − 3.","The slope is 9."],"trap":"Square x before multiplying by 3.","answer":"9"},"mth-12-08-n04":{"idea":"Derivative of ln f is f' over f.","steps":["f'(x) = 2x/(x² + 1).","At x = 1: 2/(1 + 1) = 2/2.","So f'(1) = 1."],"trap":"The numerator is the inner derivative.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-08-n05":{"idea":"The derivative of tan⁻¹x is 1/(1 + x²).","steps":["f'(x) = 1/(1 + x²).","At x = 2: 1/(1 + 4) = 1/5.","So f'(2) = 0.2."],"trap":"Square x first, then add 1.","answer":"0.2"},"mth-12-08-n06":{"idea":"Differentiate implicitly, then put in the point.","steps":["2x + 2y dy/dx = 0 gives dy/dx = −x/y.","At (3, 4): −3/4.","So the slope is −0.75."],"trap":"Keep the minus sign.","answer":"-0.75"},"mth-12-08-n07":{"idea":"For parametric curves, divide dy/dt by dx/dt.","steps":["dy/dt = 3t² = 12 at t = 2.","dx/dt = 2t = 4 at t = 2.","dy/dx = 12/4 = 3."],"trap":"y-rate over x-rate, not the other way.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-08-n08":{"idea":"Differentiate twice with the power rule.","steps":["y' = 4x³.","y'' = 12x².","At x = 2: 12 × 4 = 48."],"trap":"Differentiate twice before substituting.","answer":"48"},"mth-12-08-n09":{"idea":"Use logarithmic differentiation for xˣ.","steps":["dy/dx = xˣ(1 + ln x).","At x = 2: xˣ = 4 and ln 2 = 0.6931.","So dy/dx = 4 × 1.6931 = 6.773."],"trap":"ln 2 is about 0.693.","answer":"6.773"},"mth-12-08-n10":{"idea":"Write the tangent line and find where it meets the y-axis.","steps":["The slope at x = 3 is 2x = 6, and the point is (3, 9).","The tangent is y − 9 = 6(x − 3), which simplifies to y = 6x − 9.","Put x = 0 to get the y-intercept −9."],"trap":"The intercept is negative here.","answer":"-9"}};
